  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAfter a relatively quiet weather day courtesy of a weak high \npressure system lifting in from the south and a more zonal flow\nregime today focus turns to a more active weather pattern for \nthe new work week with multiple days of heavy shower and \nthunderstorm activity anticipated. \n\nOn Monday, we have a severe risk for strong to damaging wind \nsevere storms and heavy downpours which can cause tree or \nproperty damage and even risk lives from falling trees and/or \nfrequent lightning as well as isolated flash flooding which is \nalways dangerous to property and lives. By late morning, a weak \nMCS is anticipated by Hi-res models and the medium range models \nto impact the area. \n\nWith sufficient CAPE and DCAPE with high PWATS near the 1.5 \ninch mark we can expect some heavy downpour activity and \nstronger storms to pass through. These storms could produce a \ndamaging wind threat as well as flooding. Weak steering flow \nwill attribute to the flooding threat through the afternoon. \n\nOn Tuesday, another complex is forecast in the medium range \nmodels to affect the area in the morning and through the \nmorning which could promote stronger storms and more flash \nflooding concerns. WPC has us in a slight risk for excessive \nrainfall which will support these flooding concerns. Water \nloaded forecast soundings indicate heavy rainfall potential with\nPWATs similar to Monday. \n\nDownbursts will be possible along with a chance for damaging \nwind as boundary layer flow increases in association with a \nnocturnal jet which will support stronger winds among any \ndownbursts or heavy downpours. Subsequently, steering flow is \nstronger so storms should be moving relatively faster than what \nwe have seen in the previous days which will aide in the \nsuppression of more than just isolated chances for flash \nflooding. Only low lying or flood prone areas will really be \nsusceptible to flooding issues even though a slight risk of \nexcessive rainfall will exist. \n\nOn Wednesday, another MCS is forecast by medium range models \nand is forecast to impact the area in the early morning once \nagain. Almost a mirror image of Tuesday which will have a weak \nnocturnal jet with stronger upper level steering support. \nAnother slight risk for excessive rainfall will support isolated\nflash flooding along with damaging wind potential in any \nstronger storms that may accompany the complex. \n\nWe cannot rule out diurnal storm activity as well with stronger\nsupport in the upper levels and a stronger northwesterly \nmoisture transport amid southwesterly warm air advection which \nwill cause destabilization in the lower levels for greater \ninstability. SPC has the are under a slight risk for severe \nstorms with a 15% chance already, therefore diurnal activity \ncould promote some stronger thunderstorm activity which could \nresult in damaging wind threat.\n\nIt is worth mentioning that these complexes have low certainty \non exact timing but are forecast to happen through the mornings,\nconsequently confidence is not all there in these systems even \nhappening, but there is potential with the northwesterly \ncomponent advecting in vorticity and moisture to the area under \nstronger boundary layer flow and the help of weak and subtle \nupper level short wave trough support. \n\nMore focus then turns to Thursday Through Friday morning as a \nbroad low pressure system is forecast to traverse across the \nGreat Lakes and in lower Canada which will drag through a weak \ncold front. Models do not have a great handle on this feature \nbut overall consensus is that this boundary will support and \nimpact the area with shower and thunderstorm chances. \n\nIt is not know as this time if any severe weather will be a \nthreat, however with fast moving flow the potential for flash \nflooding will also be low as well but any training of storms \ncould provide enough rainfall to localized areas to maybe cause \nsome instances of flash flooding. WPC does have some of the area\nunder a marginal threat for excessive rainfall during this time\nframe.\n\nWith frontal passage expected on Friday, the rest of the day \nalong with the weekend looks to be a lot more drier and settled\nas high pressure settles into the area, but with models hinting\non more activity during the weekend than previous forecast \nruns, confidence in this turning out to be a settled weekend is\nlow at this time. For this reason, chances for precipitation \nwill be left into the extended forecast to represent the hint of\nthis model solution amid northwesterly flow around the \naforementioned high pressure system which could promote \nnumerous complexes again to impact the area so there will be \nchances of this overall pattern that we are in extending into \nthe weekend.",
